import mundane theorem    stdlib/sums/sum-extended
import mundane theorem    stdlib/sums/sum-single

theorem pronic-sum
  note        By induction on n: adding (k + 1)(k + 2) to k(k + 1)(k + 2)/3
              gives (k + 1)(k + 2)(k + 3)/3.
  let n ∈ ℕ                                                           (H1)
  then Σ(k = 1 to n) k·(k + 1) = n·(n + 1)·(n + 2)/3

let S(m) = Σ(j = 1 to m) j·(j + 1), for m ∈ ℕ                         (D1)
       reads the sum of the first m products j·(j + 1)

1.  For all k ∈ ℕ, S(k) = k·(k + 1)·(k + 2)/3.
    induction on k starting at 1

    base
    1.1.  S(1) = 1·(1 + 1)·(1 + 2)/3
          calculation
            S(1) = Σ(j = 1 to 1) j·(j + 1)       D1
                 = 1·(1 + 1)                     mun:sum-single a := 1
                 = 1·(1 + 1)·(1 + 2)/3           arithmetic

    step
    let k ∈ ℕ                                                         (K)
    assume step 1 is true for k, the induction hypothesis             (IH)

    1.2.  S(k + 1) = S(k) + (k + 1)·((k + 1) + 1)
          calculation
            S(k + 1) = Σ(j = 1 to k + 1) j·(j + 1)                        D1
                     = Σ(j = 1 to k) j·(j + 1) + (k + 1)·((k + 1) + 1)    mun:sum-extended a := 1, n := k
                     = S(k) + (k + 1)·((k + 1) + 1)                       D1
          requires k ≥ 1: from K

    1.3.  S(k + 1) = (k + 1)·((k + 1) + 1)·((k + 1) + 2)/3
          calculation
            S(k + 1) = S(k) + (k + 1)·((k + 1) + 1)                                1.2
                     = k·(k + 1)·(k + 2)/3 + (k + 1)·((k + 1) + 1)                 substitute IH
                     = (k + 1)·((k + 1) + 1)·((k + 1) + 2)/3                       algebra

2.  Σ(k = 1 to n) k·(k + 1) = n·(n + 1)·(n + 2)/3
    calculation
      Σ(k = 1 to n) k·(k + 1) = S(n)                    D1
                              = n·(n + 1)·(n + 2)/3     instantiate k := n in line 1
